# 1. Audit scope and reading guide

This document describes Flood Fill 3D as inspected on **25 November 2026**, version **1.0** of this functionality document. It is written for a client, product owner, or designer rather than for a programmer. A rule described as active is connected to the current 3D game, not merely represented by an asset in the project.

The inspection covered the enabled startup scene, the authored menu and gameplay screens, their saved configuration and overrides, level and goal tuning, geometry generation, the solver, budgets, scoring, ratings, local persistence, audio, camera controls, victory presentation, and the development tools. The older two-dimensional demo is disabled in the build list. Third-party example scenes and an installed package are not evidence that a corresponding feature is available in this game.

The document distinguishes three kinds of evidence:

| Evidence | What it establishes | What it does not establish |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Current rules and saved configuration | The behavior and balancing values the current game requests | That every device or interruption sequence has been tested |
| A locally saved numbered-level definition | The exact preserved puzzle, allowance, goals, and winning sequence for that local level | That every fresh installation will discover the same solver result |
| Read-only reconstruction of that winning sequence | Consistent region counts, capture progress, and completion under the inspected rules | A recorded human play session or historical random score |

No gameplay was changed, no saved progress was reset, and no new game session was launched for this documentation task. The worked example uses an inspected local Level 1 definition. Its move/capture sequence is reconstructed from that definition; its score draws are explicitly illustrative. Device performance, listening quality, app-store behavior, and operating-system interruption behavior are not newly certified by this audit.

**Important terminology:** a gold star is a move-efficiency rating. A cyan diamond is a permanently earned optional-goal achievement. Neither is the same as the displayed coin/gem reward cards, which are placeholders. “Captured” refers to the currently active connected component, not necessarily every cube ever included in an earlier component.

<a id="chapter-2"></a>

# 2. Game overview

**Flood Fill 3D** is a single-player spatial color puzzle presented as “A COLORFUL SPATIAL PUZZLE.” The player chooses a paint color, then taps or clicks a cube. All face-connected cubes of the clicked cube's existing color change together. Matching neighboring colors merge into a larger connected region. The primary objective is to make the entire playable object one connected color before the move allowance is exhausted.

The six available colors are green, purple, yellow, orange, blue, and cyan. There is no difficulty-based reduction or expansion of that palette. Color selection is accessible, while the spatial layout, limited allowance, and optional goals create the strategic challenge.

Unlike a flat board, the object has front, back, side, upper, and lower surfaces. The player must orbit it to discover useful connections and avoid leaving small regions out of sight. Empty space does not transmit a connection, and touching corners does not count. A seemingly useful choice on one face can change what becomes accessible on another.

The game is a **free-region flood puzzle**: the player may recolor any reachable cube's region, not only a fixed starting region. This creates an additional strategic choice—continue enlarging the present region, or work on another region to create a better future merge. Previous recolorings remain on the board, but the capture indicator switches to the component of the latest clicked cube.

Replay incentives are higher gold-star ratings, a lower saved move count, a faster saved numbered-level completion time, and missing optional-goal diamonds. The live score offers moment-to-moment feedback, but there is no implemented permanent best-score leaderboard. Android and iOS are the intended mobile targets; desktop mouse controls and Editor play are supported. This audit does not establish a released mobile build or store availability.

