# 3. Complete player journey

On launch, the game presents the main menu and checks local saved data. If a valid unfinished attempt exists, CONTINUE becomes available; the player is not automatically sent into that attempt. The menu normally displays a rotating decorative cube cluster. With SHOW CURRENT BLOCK enabled and a board available, it can instead display the actual current board as a menu preview.

A normal numbered-level journey is:

1. Press PLAY to open LEVELS.
2. Choose FULL GRID, RANDOM SHAPE, or ITEMS.
3. Choose EASY, MEDIUM, HARD, or EXPERT.
4. Choose an unlocked numbered level. The first level of every category is initially available.
5. Wait through preparation. A new numbered level needs a board, a verified solution, an allowance, and optional goals; an already saved level restores its preserved definition.
6. Watch the brief board entrance. Cube and color input remain unavailable until the entrance is complete.
7. Select a palette color, then tap a cube. Rotate, pan, or zoom to inspect other surfaces as necessary.
8. Watch moves used, elapsed active-play time, score, and the capture-progress bar. Open GOALS to inspect optional targets.
9. Continue until the board is completely connected or the allowance is used up.
10. On a win, receive the current attempt's gold-star rating, save any newly earned goal achievements, save better move/time records, and unlock the next level in that category.
11. Choose NEXT LEVEL, REPLAY, HOME, or LEVELS. At Level 100, the primary action is LEVELS instead of creating Level 101.

For example, a new player can choose Full Grid/Easy/1, learn the six-color interaction on a 6×6×6 shell, finish a verified puzzle, and unlock Full Grid/Easy/2. This does not unlock Random Shape/Easy/2 or Full Grid/Medium/2. Those categories have independent progression.

Choosing a color, selecting a level, and navigating require player interaction. Generation, validation, capture calculation, score awards, goal tracking, result evaluation, and local completion saving happen automatically. A new level does not play itself; automated solving belongs to a separate development tool.

