# 5. Game modes and additional play routes

### Full Grid

Full Grid is a hollow cube made from its one-cube-thick outer shell. The interior is not playable geometry. For side length n, playable cubes equal **n³ − (n−2)³**. The shape is regular and remains fixed for a given dimension. Colors are distributed independently over the playable cubes, producing connected color groups by chance rather than by a separate clustering rule.

Opposite faces can connect around the cube's edges, but cannot connect through the empty center. The challenge is to plan across all six surfaces, including small regions hidden behind the object.

### Random Shape

Random Shape first grows a connected solid within a cubic envelope, then identifies its externally exposed skin. Only the largest face-connected surface component is used as playable geometry. Growth is biased toward a center and several lobes, giving irregular outlines rather than simply deleting random cubes from a full grid.

The player sees an irregular object with gaps and protrusions. Internal solid volume and sealed interior cavities are not separate hidden objectives. Surface extraction can leave several skin components even when the temporary solid was connected; retaining the largest playable component is the safeguard against an inaccessible separate island. The final playable surface therefore can contain fewer cubes than either the envelope capacity or the temporary grown solid.

The present scene uses 44–62% solid fill before size simplification, two to three lobes, and three generation attempts at most. Those percentages describe the temporary solid, not the final percentage of playable surface cubes. Large shapes are deliberately made more compact and less intricate as their envelope increases.

### Items

Numbered ITEMS levels use procedurally formed fruit silhouettes: **Apple, Pear, Orange, and Banana**. These are geometric voxel silhouettes, not imported finished colored models. Apple has broad shoulders and a dimple; Pear has a broad lower body and narrower upper portion; Orange is approximately spherical; Banana is a curved tapered tube. Their visible skins are extracted and the largest connected skin is retained.

The level seed selects one of four silhouettes. With the current seed schedule, numbered Items levels repeat Apple, Pear, Orange, Banana in that order, starting at Level 1, independently of difficulty. The silhouette repeats, but size bands and color layouts change. Very low-resolution or narrow features can lose detached tips during connectivity cleanup; the game prioritizes a single playable object over preserving every detail.

The custom setup's FRUITS menu is **not** this active Items entry point. It is marked COMING SOON and does not start a custom fruit puzzle.

| Mode | Geometry and generation | Numbered dimensions | Main strategic distinction | Difficulty influence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| FULL GRID | Regular outer shell | 6 through 20, in seven size bands | Connections around six faces and edges | Different seed/allowance/goals; no special shape rule |
| RANDOM SHAPE | Controlled connected growth, exterior skin, largest component | Same seven bands | Uneven surfaces, gaps, branches and visibility | Different seed/allowance/goals; shared shape tuning |
| ITEMS | Four analytic fruit skins, largest connected component | Same seven bands | Curved outlines and narrow connecting areas | Different seed/allowance/goals; same silhouette cycle |

### Additional routes and older content

The current main PLAY flow is the 1,200-level catalog. There is also an older sequential campaign reachable indirectly through NEXT LEVEL after winning a custom game. It starts from its separately remembered campaign position, uses Full Grid on most stages and Random Shape on every fifth stage, and progresses through the seven sizes in groups of five. Beyond its first 35 stages it chooses a size for the current campaign stage and remembers that choice. It has no numbered-catalog secondary goals. This route is a current navigation inconsistency, not another 1,200-level category.

An older 2D game remains in the project but is not enabled as the startup build scene. Third-party water, terrain, and other demonstration content are not active Flood Fill 3D modes.

