# 7. Numbered levels and progression

There are **3 modes × 4 difficulties × 100 levels = 1,200 numbered levels**, arranged in 12 independent categories. A level is identified by all three choices: mode, difficulty, and number. Full Grid/Easy/10 and Random Shape/Hard/10 are different puzzles with separate completion, stars, goal achievements, records, and unlock chains.

Level 1 is unlocked in every category. A later level is available when its immediately preceding level is completed, or when that later level already has a completion record. Winning is sufficient; optional goals and a particular gold-star count are not required. Failure does not unlock the following level.

| Numbered level range | Envelope dimensions |
| --- | --- |
| 1–15 | 6×6×6 |
| 16–30 | 8×8×8 |
| 31–45 | 10×10×10 |
| 46–60 | 12×12×12 |
| 61–75 | 15×15×15 |
| 76–90 | 18×18×18 |
| 91–100 | 20×20×20 |

LEVELS shows 30 tiles per page: 1–30, 31–60, 61–90, and 91–100. The last page contains ten tiles, not thirty additional levels. Left/right arrows and four page dots navigate. The active dot is larger and cyan; boundary arrows are non-interactable. The last manually chosen page is remembered separately for each category while that selection screen instance exists, rather than being a guaranteed persistent preference across application launches.

Locked tiles show a lock. Available tiles show their number and rating/goal placeholders. Completed tiles retain their best gold stars and earned goal count. The highlighted tile is the first incomplete level in that category; after all 100 are complete it remains Level 100. That highlight is not a promise that an unfinished checkpoint belongs to that tile.

The category counter is completed levels out of 100. Because the denominator is exactly 100, 19 completed means 19%, regardless of their star or diamond totals. Replaying a completed level does not increment that count again.

The catalog keeps the lowest winning move count, highest earned gold-star count, and best recorded numbered completion time. It does not keep a permanent best score. At Level 100, victory's primary button becomes LEVELS and the duplicate lower LEVELS button is hidden; HOME is centered without an empty button slot.

