# 8. Procedural board generation

A level is generated under constraints rather than as unrestricted random noise. The preparation flow is:

    Select a level
        ↓
    Restore its existing saved definition, if valid
        OR
    Choose its envelope and seed → construct playable geometry → assign colors
        ↓
    Analyze connected regions → find and validate a winning sequence
        ↓
    Calculate the allowance → generate compatible optional goals → save the definition
        ↓
    Prepare the view → show the entrance → enable play

Full Grid creates its regular outer shell directly. Items creates one of the four fruit solids and extracts a connected skin. Random Shape grows from a seed location, adding neighboring material so the temporary solid stays connected. Growth can favor lobes, centerward positions, brushes of adjacent material, or branches. The scene's base growth chances are brush 35%, branch 18%, directional persistence 19%, center bias 41%, lobe strength 44%, with six weighted candidates per choice and two to three attractors/lobes. These are generation controls, not player probabilities of winning.

Random Shape has no outer margin configured and requires a span of at least four cubes in each axis where the envelope permits. It targets an integer solid population between the ceiling of the minimum fill count and floor of the maximum fill count. It flood-checks outside air and extracts material touching that outside air. Sealed internal cavities are not treated as exterior playable surfaces. The largest face-connected surface component is retained, and the final bounds must still meet the minimum spans.

At most three attempts are made. The third attempt avoids the brush feature; optional notch carving is only attempted on the first attempt when enabled. **Notches are currently disabled**, so their configured 12% chance and one pass are not active gameplay behavior. If irregular generation fails its attempts, the level uses a hollow cube fallback instead of hanging or presenting a disconnected puzzle. A level still passes through solver and numbered-definition validation after that geometric fallback.

For large Random Shape envelopes, let q = 0.65 × clamp((n−8)/12, 0, 1). The solid-fill range becomes 0.44 + 0.08q through 0.62 + 0.05q. At size 20, q is 0.65 and the range is approximately **49.2–65.25%**. Brush growth and central compactness increase, while branching, directional persistence, and lobe strength decrease. This size-dependent simplification applies across difficulties.

| Side length | Envelope capacity | Full Grid playable shell |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 6 | 216 | 152 |
| 8 | 512 | 296 |
| 10 | 1,000 | 488 |
| 12 | 1,728 | 728 |
| 15 | 3,375 | 1,178 |
| 18 | 5,832 | 1,736 |
| 20 | 8,000 | 2,168 |

Random Shape and Items counts vary and should not be inferred from this shell table. A 20³ envelope does not imply 8,000 playable cubes. More geometry and more distinct connected color regions can increase preparation cost, but an irregular thin object may have fewer playable cubes than a smaller-looking regular shell.

Initial colors are selected independently from the six-color palette. Numbered levels use a seed-derived color sequence. The initial active component is the face-connected color group containing a deterministic starting cube. It counts toward capture progress but earns no initial score. That starting component is a reference point, not a restriction on which region the player may manipulate next.

<a id="chapter-9"></a>

# 9. Seeds, preserved puzzles, and deterministic replay

A seed is a repeatable input to generation. Numbered identities use a fixed seed schedule. With modes numbered Full Grid 0, Random Shape 1, Items 2, and difficulties Easy 0 through Expert 3:

**Category position = 100 × (4 × mode + difficulty) + level − 1**

**Seed = 1,179,005,764 + 104,729 × category position**

The identity uses numbers internally, but players only need the three visible choices. For example, Full Grid/Easy/1 has seed **1,179,005,764**. Each distinct catalog identity has a different scheduled seed.

More importantly, the first successful preparation saves the **actual initial occupied coordinates and cube colors**, palette, seed, allowance, optional goals, a verified winning sequence, and generation/version information. Replay therefore restores the preserved puzzle rather than depending on regenerating it from a seed alone. Its move allowance and goal definitions are also restored, even if a later search could discover a better solution.

The current attempt is saved separately. Restarting a numbered level discards the attempt's changed colors, moves, score, elapsed time, and temporary goal progress, then reloads the original preserved definition. Permanent completion, best stars, best moves/time, and previously earned goals remain.

The board-generation rules and seed are expected to reproduce an initial numbered board under the same implementation. **Identical budgets and goals on different fresh installations are not guaranteed:** solver refinements and goal-candidate collection are time-bounded, and different search outcomes can change the retained witness. Once a device has a valid definition, its saved replay is the stronger guarantee. There is no server distributing one universal puzzle file to every player.

Saved definitions are versioned and checked against their board integrity and witness. They are not silently overwritten when a definition already exists. If a future update makes an old definition unsupported, the current handling is to preserve the file and show a loading error, not silently substitute a new puzzle. Compatibility across arbitrary future generation updates is therefore not guaranteed.

Custom games have no equivalent immutable numbered definition. Full Grid keeps the same regular geometry at the chosen size but can receive new colors on restart. Random Shape normally gets a fresh seed and geometry on restart. Continuing an unfinished custom game restores its current saved board; starting or restarting a custom game is a different action.

<a id="chapter-10"></a>

# 10. Core gameplay rules and practical examples

A valid move has two parts: choose a palette color, then click or tap a playable cube whose current color differs from that chosen paint. Every cube face-connected to the clicked cube through its original color is recolored. Neighboring regions of the new color then join the resulting component, including all matching regions reached through chains of face adjacency.

There are six possible neighbor directions: left, right, up, down, front, and back. An edge-only or corner-only touch does not connect cubes. A gap does not connect them. Cubes on different visible faces can nevertheless meet across the physical edge of the object through adjacent grid positions.

**Example:** five connected purple cubes touch six connected yellow cubes. Selecting yellow and clicking one of the purple cubes changes all five purple cubes to yellow. The resulting yellow component contains eleven cubes if those two groups are the only connected material involved. If the active component before that move was those five purple cubes, the newly captured count is six. If the player instead moved from a separate active component, more of those eleven may count as newly active.

**Working elsewhere is allowed.** The player can select orange and recolor a remote green region even while a large purple component exists elsewhere. The green region changes, the previous purple paint remains, and the active capture component becomes the component at the newly clicked cube. Capture progress can consequently decrease. There is no permanent ownership of every cube previously called “captured.”

Newly captured cubes are the cubes in the new active component that were not in the immediately preceding active component. They are not the increase in a lifetime total. Revisiting regions can therefore count cubes again after they have left the active component. This definition affects score and optional capture goals.

| Interaction | Consumes a move? | Consequence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Choose a different palette button | No | Changes the paint selection; may count toward a color-selection goal |
| Choose the already selected palette color | No | Ignored; the selected palette button is disabled |
| Click a cube already matching the selected paint | No | No recolor and no score |
| Click a different-color region without touching any selected-color neighbor | Yes | Recolors that component even without an external merge |
| Recolor another independent region | Yes | Changes the active component and can lower the capture bar |
| Click outside a cube, or release a camera drag | No | Does not become a paint move |
| Attempt play while loading, paused, in entrance animation, or after the result | No | Gameplay input is blocked |

The color-state change and move/capture calculations happen immediately when accepted. The wave is a presentation of that already accepted state. Normal recolor waves do not lock all subsequent input; rapid valid moves can be accepted and replace affected visual transitions. The original wave is therefore not a required wait between every move.

Victory means the active connected component contains **every playable cube**. A visually near-complete board is not sufficient. This is checked before the move-limit loss check, so completing the board on the final available move is a win.

<a id="chapter-11"></a>

# 11. Player controls and camera behavior

| Action | Touch | Mouse |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Choose paint | Tap a palette button | Click a palette button |
| Apply paint | Tap a cube without a drag | Click/release on a cube without a drag |
| Orbit | One-finger drag outside blocking UI | Left-button drag |
| Pan | Two-finger movement together | Right- or middle-button drag |
| Zoom | Change two-finger separation | Mouse wheel |
| Restore framing | Double tap | Double click/tap-style left interaction |

The board selection gesture uses a 12-pixel drag threshold to distinguish a tap from an orbit gesture. A gesture that begins on a color control or other blocking UI does not turn into an unintended cube paint. Camera gestures are also checked against UI. Multi-touch movement is not interpreted as a paint click.

The current scene limits pitch to −75° through +75°. Fresh framing begins at approximately −40° yaw and 28° pitch. The camera fits the board's enclosing volume to the available aspect ratio and uses proportional padding of 1.05, so larger boards and narrow portrait screens can require more distance. It can expand the nominal maximum zoom-out distance to fit a large board safely.

The nominal distance limits are 4 and 30 world units, but the outside-board safety rule can push the actual minimum or maximum beyond them. The inspected scene's surface-clearance value is **14 world units**; an earlier prose note described 0.75, but that is not the current saved scene value. With cubes one world unit wide, 14 is a substantial clearance and may make the initial view or zoom-in limit more distant than expected. This audit records the value rather than changing it.

For balancing reference, the saved scene uses orbit sensitivity 0.2, pan sensitivity 5, and zoom sensitivity 15. These are response settings rather than player-facing speed numbers. Panning scales with camera distance and screen height. Two-finger zoom normalizes movement to screen size.

Double-tap recognition uses a maximum 0.32-second gap, a 36-pixel movement allowance per tap, and an 80-pixel separation allowance. The reset smoothly restores the original center and fitted zoom over 0.45 seconds while keeping the present viewing angle. A new pan or zoom interrupts the reset. A fresh/replayed numbered level is framed again; Continue restores the saved view subject to current safety limits.

Pause, its Settings screen, and Home confirmation stop camera interaction. Board entrance and loading also block it. The floating goals panel consumes an outside tap used to dismiss it so that tap does not paint a cube; an outside drag can close the panel and remain available for camera movement.

<a id="chapter-12"></a>

# 12. Solver: what it verifies and how it searches

The solver exists to demonstrate that a new puzzle has a winning route and to establish a sensible move allowance. Normal players do not have to watch or use it directly. It works during preparation and is bypassed when a valid preserved numbered definition already supplies its winning route.

Conceptually, the solver compresses every initially connected same-color group into a region. It remembers each region's color, cube count, and neighboring regions. It then studies recoloring any region and merging matching neighbors. This matches free-region play, rather than assuming every move must grow a fixed corner.

Its current search combines several strategies:

| Strategy | Plain-language role | Limitation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Initial monotone completion | Establishes a complete route by repeatedly joining regions before trying to improve it | A safe first route is not necessarily short |
| Multiple greedy strategies | Try moves with immediate benefits: merged cube mass, number of joined groups, or useful frontier exposure | A locally attractive move can be poor later |
| Memoized lookahead | Compare a few moves ahead and reuse evaluations of repeated positions | Bounded depth/branching cannot inspect every future |
| Diverse free-region beam search | Retain a limited collection of promising partial routes, including different opening choices | Discarded alternatives may contain a better route |
| Anchored beam search | Also grow from the starting region and up to three promising large/well-connected origins; compare frontier distance and future merges | This is one additional search route, not a restriction on player moves |
| Iterative-deepening A* (IDA*) | Search routes below the current best length using a mathematically safe lower bound, increasing the permitted depth | Runs only for suitably small region counts and within limits |
| Independent replay validation | Apply the complete candidate sequence under the flood rules and confirm that all regions end in one color | Proves that route works, not that no shorter route exists |

Greedy ranking tries six variants. For example, a move that joins six cubes immediately can outrank one joining four under a mass-focused strategy; another strategy can prefer the four-cube choice if it joins more separate regions or exposes a stronger frontier. The actual ranking coefficients are included in Chapter 35. Such scores rank search choices; they are not the player's score.

The solver remembers duplicate positions, rejects candidates that cannot improve the retained route, and uses memory-aware beam widths. It keeps a validated incumbent, meaning the best route demonstrated so far, even if a refinement phase stops early. Shorter candidates are accepted only after validation. The minimum number of globally distinct colors minus one supplies a safe lower bound; a more detailed region estimate is used for ranking but is not misrepresented as a proof.

Current normal-play refinement settings are six greedy runs, beam width 128, lookahead depth 3, branch limit 2, and lookahead node limit 128. Greedy and beam phases each have a 200 ms budget. Exact search is eligible at 128 initial regions or fewer and has a 40 ms budget and nominal 100,000-state limit. Search memory guidance is 32 MB, which can further reduce effective widths/state limits. These are phase controls, not a hard total loading deadline: building the graph, establishing the first completion, validating it, and other preparation also take time.

The developer analysis tool can use larger budgets; ordinary play uses normal-play budgets even in Editor play and development builds. All retained routes are validated by the shared rule model. A fallback may establish a longer monotone route if refinement fails. Numbered content is not published when no within-budget validated witness can be prepared.

Four outcomes must remain distinct:

1. **Valid solution:** this particular sequence wins.
2. **Best-known solution:** the shortest validated sequence found during this search.
3. **Proven optimal solution:** the search or a matching safe lower bound proves that a shorter route does not exist.
4. **Inconclusive search:** available search effort did not establish the desired result; stopping is not proof of impossibility.

Most large boards can be validated without being proven optimal. The game does not promise that every allowance comes from the mathematical minimum, or that a later deeper analysis will rediscover the same or a shorter route within its own time limit.

<a id="chapter-13"></a>

# 13. Move allowance calculation

Let **S** be the retained validated solution length, with a minimum of 1, and **R** the initial connected color-region count. Let **round** mean nearest whole number, with exact halves rounded upward for these non-negative values. The game rounds the percentage contribution first and the complexity-scaled contribution second.

| Initial regions R | Complexity multiplier C |
| --- | ---: |
| Fewer than 80 | 1.00 |
| 80–149 | 1.10 |
| 150–249 | 1.25 |
| 250–399 | 1.40 |
| 400 or more | 1.60 |

Calculate:

- Hard extra = round(max(1, round(0.08 × S)) × C), at least 1.
- Medium extra = round(max(3, round(0.23 × S)) × C), at least 1.
- Easy extra = round(max(5, round(0.38 × S)) × C), at least 1.
- Expert allowance = S.
- Hard allowance = at least Expert + 1, using S + Hard extra.
- Medium allowance = at least Hard + 1, using S + Medium extra.
- Easy allowance = at least Medium + 1, using S + Easy extra.

The configured upper allowance is 1,000,000 moves. To preserve strict tier ordering it leaves two positions below the cap for Hard, one for Medium, and none for Easy. The effective ceiling is at least S + 3, even if a lower ceiling were configured. Current supported puzzles are far below that ceiling; it is not a normal target allowance. There is no separate fixed board-size bonus: board size influences the solution and region count instead.

**Worked example:** S = 14 and R = 101 give C = 1.1. Hard's 8% contribution rounds to 1, then remains 1 after scaling. Medium's 23% rounds to 3, then remains 3. Easy's 38% rounds to 5; 5 × 1.1 = 5.5 rounds to 6. The allowances are Expert 14, Hard 15, Medium 17, and Easy 20.

| S, with R = 101 | EASY | MEDIUM / NORMAL | HARD | EXPERT / PERFECT |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 5 | 11 | 8 | 6 | 5 |
| 10 | 16 | 13 | 11 | 10 |
| 14 | 20 | 17 | 15 | 14 |
| 20 | 29 | 26 | 22 | 20 |

These comparisons apply the same mathematical board statistics to each tier; actual numbered identities at different difficulties can have different seeds and therefore different S and R. Two boards of equal dimensions can receive different allowances because their region layouts differ.

For a newly published numbered level, the retained winning witness is no longer than its allowance. Saved replay restores that allowance rather than recalculating it. Optional goals do not add or subtract ordinary moves. A developer's later search can return an over-budget plan without invalidating the original stored witness; that later plan simply cannot be played to completion under the current limit.

For custom play only, if no validated preparation result is available, the emergency allowance uses max(1, playable cube count − 1) as a conservative solution-length input to the same tier calculation. This is a recovery bound based on merging connected material, not a saved demonstrated optimal sequence. A numbered level instead stops loading with an error when no validated definition can be established.

<a id="chapter-14"></a>

# 14. Moves, capture progress, and the active-play clock

**Remaining moves = max(0, allowance − moves used).** The HUD displays used / allowance, such as 7 / 20. It updates on accepted moves; choosing paint alone does not consume a move. Once a move wins or exhausts the allowance, further color/cube gameplay input is disabled immediately, before the result popup finishes appearing.

**Capture percentage = 100 × active connected cubes / total playable cubes.** Initially connected cubes count. For a 152-cube board with one active cube, the true starting fraction is about 0.66%. The current mobile HUD's bar uses the actual fraction; it is not limited to cubes gained after the first move. The center HUD box is TIME ELAPSED, not a duplicate numeric capture percentage.

The capture bar animates toward the accepted logical result over 0.25 seconds. It does not wait for every cube in the visual wave before calculating progress. Because players can manipulate another region, both its target and the active capture count can go down. The ordinary whole-number capture display, where used, caps incomplete boards at 99 and reserves 100 for actual full capture. Detailed failure text also shows the actual captured/total count and a capped fractional percentage so a nearly complete failed board is not presented as a true 100% win.

**Elapsed time is active-play time.** It accumulates only after preparation, while gameplay interaction is allowed, the application is not suspended, and game time is running. Loading, the initial entrance, pause, the pause Settings screen, Home confirmation, and completed-result screens do not add to it. Normal color waves do add time because they do not lock the next move. The display drops fractional seconds and uses mm:ss, switching to hh:mm:ss at one hour. It is not a countdown.

The clock survives Continue and resets to zero on restart/new play. It is saved at checkpoints, not written to disk on every displayed second. Losing application focus triggers a save, but focus loss alone is not an explicit in-game pause request. Actual app suspension stops the timer and skips an oversized first tick when returning; device lifecycle details still require platform testing.

Running out of moves with even one disconnected cube is a loss. Completing the final cube on the last move is a win because completion is evaluated first.

<a id="chapter-15"></a>

# 15. Complete scoring system

The initial score is zero. Points are awarded after an accepted recolor only when the newly active cube count is positive. The reward is **not** a fixed number per cube and is **not** a fixed number per move.

For each scoring move, choose an integer base **B uniformly from 21 through 49**, inclusive. Let **N** be newly captured cubes relative to the previous active component. The multiplier is:

| N | Multiplier |
| --- | ---: |
| 0 | No award or random draw |
| 1–3 | 1 |
| 4–6 | 3 |
| 7 | 4 |
| 8 | 5 |
| 9 | 6 |
| 10 or more | 7 |

**Move points = B × multiplier.** The total score adds these awards. For example, with a base draw of 30, capturing 3 new cubes gives 30 points, 5 gives 90, 8 gives 150, and either 10 or 100 gives 210. The multiplier has a cap, so doubling a large capture does not necessarily double its score. There is no multiplier of 2 in this rule.

The randomness means two identical winning move sequences can finish with different scores. Chapter 34 uses valid illustrative draws to show a mathematically consistent total; it does not claim those draws were the historical saved result.

“New” is relative to the immediately preceding active component. It is not a global once-per-level collection record. Recoloring within the same component without acquiring cubes can give no points, while choosing a different component can make its cubes newly active and award points even without increasing the board's largest connected region. Repeated region switching can count cubes again if they left and later reentered the active component. The move allowance remains the constraint on such play.

There is currently no implemented score contribution for speed, spare moves, difficulty, mode, board dimension, consecutive combos, gold stars, optional-goal completion, or the final win itself. There is no explicit score deduction for an unproductive move; it simply spends a move and may award nothing. No designed score ceiling is enforced beyond the numeric storage used by the game.

The current score is checkpointed for Continue and appears during play and in the failure result. Restart/new play resets it. The catalog does not store a best score, and the current victory popup emphasizes moves, BEST moves, and time rather than a final-score box. The decorative +100/+5 reward cards do not alter this score.

<a id="chapter-16"></a>

# 16. Secondary goals: purpose and all goal types

Secondary goals are optional challenges attached to a preserved numbered level. The generator requests up to three, but can publish fewer, including none, when appropriate validated candidates are unavailable. Their definitions are fixed on replay. Custom games and the indirectly reachable older campaign do not receive these numbered optional goals.

A goal may be provisionally met during play, but **a win is required to earn it permanently**. Missing a goal does not make the level fail, reduce the move allowance, or prevent unlocking the next level. A player can collect different achievements on different successful attempts.

The following ten goal types are implemented. Players see the descriptive instruction, not necessarily the short category label used here.

| Goal category | Player instruction and progress | When it can no longer succeed in that attempt | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Move Saver | Finish within a target number of moves; tracks moves used | Once the target is exceeded | Finish within 17 moves |
| Final Color | Win with the entire active object in the specified color | Evaluated at the win; it is not earned just by selecting that color earlier | Finish with orange |
| Big Capture | Make at least one move with the target newly active cube count; tracks the best such count | Not inherently missed until the attempt ends | Capture 9 new cubes in one move |
| Quick Start | Reach the specified percentage/cube target within an early move deadline; retains the best early count | At its deadline if the target has not been reached | Capture 25% within 4 moves |
| Combo Capture | Make the requested number of qualifying capture moves; these need not be consecutive | Not inherently missed until the attempt ends | Capture 8+ new cubes on 3 moves |
| Speed Run | Win within an active-play time target | The goal becomes missed when time exceeds its target, but the level remains playable | Finish within 120 seconds |
| Limited Color Usage | Select the named palette color at most the target number of times | When actual palette selections exceed the limit | Select purple at most 2 times |
| Final Sprint | After first reaching the stated capture threshold, win within the specified number of additional moves | Once more than that many moves have elapsed since the crossing | After reaching 70%, finish in 3 more moves |
| Fast Expansion | A later reach-percentage deadline, using the same progress rule as Quick Start | At its deadline if the target has not been reached | Capture 50% within 7 moves |
| Final Color Efficiency | Win in the specified color within a move target | Exceeding the move target, or finishing in another color | Finish with cyan within 15 moves |

Final Sprint counts moves **after** the first threshold-crossing move. The crossing move itself is not one of the additional moves. Dropping below the threshold later does not restart its countdown. Big Capture and Combo Capture use the same active-component difference count used by score, not a lifetime unique-cube total.

Limited Color Usage measures palette choices, not uses of paint on cubes. Keeping one selected color for several legal recolors counts as one selection. Switching to the named color and never applying it still counts. Pressing the already selected color is ignored and does not count again.

All goal types are constrained by the mode/difficulty selection pools in Chapter 17. Final Color Efficiency is implemented as a rule but is not ordinarily generated with the current configuration: its only positive selection weights are on Expert, where a zero-spare allowance fails its required one-move saving condition. A theoretically implemented type is not necessarily eligible on every difficulty. Their reward is a persistent goal achievement shown by a diamond. They do not award extra gold stars, coins, gems, score, or moves.

<a id="chapter-17"></a>

# 17. How procedural goals are selected and calculated

Goal generation studies the same verified winning route used to establish the level. Let **L** be its length, **M** the allowance, **N** total playable cubes, **I** initially active cubes, **R** initial color regions, and **d** the difficulty position 0–3. For each witness move it records newly active cubes, active component size, selected color, and the final winning color.

The minimum meaningful capture is **max(2, ceil(0.02 × N))**. Each candidate must be demonstrated by the witness. The generator considers at most 30 candidates and has a configured 30 ms candidate-generation budget; those bounds can cause fewer goals to be offered. Witness validation has a 2,048-move limit.

| Goal | Exact target construction and eligibility |
| --- | --- |
| Move Saver | T = L + floor((M−L) × spare-move fraction). Offer only if T ≤ M−1. |
| Final Color Efficiency | Same T and minimum-saving condition, plus the witness's final color. |
| Final Color | Witness final color. |
| Big Capture | Let P be the largest witness new-capture count. T = max(minimum meaningful capture, ceil(P × capture fraction)); require T ≤ P and T < N−I. |
| Quick Start | Deadline K = max(1, floor(L × early fraction)); require K < L. Take witness active size at move K, convert to percent, round down to a multiple of 5%, then convert that percent back to cubes by rounding up. Require percent between 0 and 100 and target greater than I + minimum meaningful capture. |
| Fast Expansion | Same calculation, but use min(0.7, early fraction + 0.25) for K. |
| Combo Capture | Required moves Q = max(2, ceil(L × (0.15 + 0.05d))). Sort witness new-capture counts largest first. Scale the Qth largest by the capture fraction and round up, with the meaningful-capture minimum. Offer only when Q moves can demonstrate the resulting target. These moves need not be consecutive. |
| Limited Color Usage | Consider a color occupying at least N / 6 initial cubes. It must be selected at least once in the witness, less often than the most-selected color, and at most L / 3 times. Its target is its demonstrated selection count. |
| Final Sprint | Threshold is 70% of N, rounded up to cubes. Find its first witness crossing. The additional-move deadline is the number of witness moves after that crossing. Require at least two remaining moves and that the initial component was below the threshold. |
| Speed Run | T = max(60, L × seconds-per-move + cube-root(N) × 8 + R × 0.12). Multiply by 1 for Full Grid, 1.25 for Random Shape, or 1.15 for Items; round upward to a multiple of 5 seconds. |

Capture fraction, spare-move fraction, early fraction, and seconds per move are the per-difficulty values in Chapter 6. Deadline targets are based on active component counts, including initial cubes. The witness's color-selection statistics collapse repeated unchanged selections, matching normal palette behavior.

**Timing is an estimate, not a mathematical human-play guarantee.** The structural goals are replay-validated; a Speed Run target estimates planning and camera time rather than proving that every player or device can complete it in that duration.

### Selection weights

The numbers below are relative weights, not fixed final percentages. A zero disables that goal type for that category. Actual odds also depend on which targets are valid for the board and which families have already been selected.

Abbreviations: MS = Move Saver; FC = Final Color; BC = Big Capture; QS = Quick Start; CC = Combo Capture; SR = Speed Run; LC = Limited Color Usage; FS = Final Sprint; FE = Fast Expansion; FCE = Final Color Efficiency.

| Mode / difficulty | MS | FC | BC | QS | CC | SR | LC | FS | FE | FCE |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Full Grid / Easy | 3 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Full Grid / Medium | 3 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Full Grid / Hard | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 3 | 1 | 2 | 0 | 3 | 0 |
| Full Grid / Expert | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 0 | 3 |
| Random Shape / Easy | 2 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Random Shape / Medium | 2 | 2 | 3 | 0 | 3 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 2 | 0 |
| Random Shape / Hard | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 0 |
| Random Shape / Expert | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 0 | 3 |
| Items / Easy | 2 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 0 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Items / Medium | 2 | 3 | 3 | 0 | 2 | 1 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Items / Hard | 3 | 2 | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 2 | 0 | 3 | 0 |
| Items / Expert | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 3 | 0 | 2 | 3 | 0 | 3 |

After choosing one goal, all other candidates in its family are removed. Move Saver, Final Color, and Final Color Efficiency share a family. Quick Start, Fast Expansion, and Final Sprint share another. Big Capture and Combo Capture share another. Speed Run and Limited Color Usage each have their own family. Thus a level does not get both Big Capture and Combo Capture, or both Quick Start and Final Sprint.

Expert Move Saver and Final Color Efficiency candidates cannot meet the required saving of at least one move because their target equals the allowance. Although those Expert weights are positive, they do not make these candidates eligible. Final Color Efficiency has no positive weights elsewhere, so it is a latent goal type under the current pools.

The seed drives weighted selection, but a time cutoff can change which candidates existed before the choice. Once saved, the chosen goals do not reroll. Because all non-time candidates are checked against the same witness, a selected set has a common demonstrated structural route; this is stronger than testing three unrelated goals separately. It does not convert the Speed Run estimate into a guaranteed human time.

If generation cannot produce three candidates, it saves the smaller valid set. An optional-goal generation failure can leave zero goals without changing the primary win rule. A missing within-budget witness, however, prevents publishing the numbered level.

<a id="chapter-18"></a>

# 18. Goal tracking, achievements, and repeat attempts

Goals track current-attempt progress as palette selections and accepted moves occur. Big Capture keeps the largest newly active count. Combo Capture counts each qualifying move. Early reach goals remember whether their target was met before the deadline. Time goals read the active-play clock. Move/selection limits can become impossible before the attempt ends.

A provisional cyan completed goal is not yet a permanent reward. The final result evaluates every goal again. A permanent award requires both a level win and that goal's rule, with no failed constraint. Losing after a large capture or an early deadline target does not bank those achievements. On failure, the current attempt's unearned goals are marked missed. Previously earned achievements remain intact.

On a successful numbered replay, new goal identities are added to the saved set only if absent. There is no duplicate reward for the same goal. Lower-performing replays cannot erase a previously saved diamond. Because the preserved definitions stay fixed, missing goals can be pursued on later attempts without changing the puzzle.

The GOALS button shows permanent earned goals / assigned goals. It is not a live count of every provisional success in the current attempt. Within the panel, an individual row can show a provisional success before the button's permanent count changes on a win.

**Multiple-attempt example:** on the inspected Easy Level 1, the three goals are finish within 17 moves, capture 9 new cubes on one move, and reach 25% within 4 moves. A successful 16-move route that misses the early target can permanently earn the first two. The next attempt starts with fresh move/capture counters but keeps those two diamonds. A successful 14-move replay can earn the remaining early target and improve gold stars. A later loss neither erases the three diamonds nor adds new ones.

Continue can restore current temporary goal progress when its saved history is consistent. An older or incomplete checkpoint lacking matching move history is not allowed to reconstruct and award partial-attempt goals speculatively; goal tracking may be unavailable for that resumed attempt. This protects achievements at the cost of a legacy-checkpoint limitation.

<a id="chapter-19"></a>

# 19. Gold-star ratings

Gold stars measure move efficiency only. Let **U** be moves used and **M** the allowance, with a minimum denominator of 1.

**Efficiency ratio = U / M**

- Three stars: ratio ≤ 0.70.
- Two stars: ratio > 0.70 and ≤ 0.90.
- One star: ratio > 0.90, provided the level was won.
- Failure: no new completion rating.

Score, elapsed time, mode, difficulty name, and optional diamonds do not otherwise enter this calculation. The rating compares the fraction directly; it does not round a displayed percentage before rating. For whole-number move counts, the practical boundaries are usually floor(0.70M) and floor(0.90M).

| Allowance | Three-star winning moves | Two-star winning moves | One-star winning moves |
| ---: | --- | --- | --- |
| 7 | 1–4 | 5–6 | 7 |
| 11 | 1–7 | 8–9 | 10–11 |
| 17 | 1–11 | 12–15 | 16–17 |
| 20 | 1–14 | 15–18 | 19–20 |

These are rating ranges, not claims that every board has a solution in every range. Very small allowances can have empty intermediate star bands. A two-move allowance, for example, gives three stars for a one-move win and one star for a two-move win, with no attainable two-star move count.

For the same 14-move route in Chapter 13, Easy's allowance of 20 yields three stars; Medium's 17 yields two; Hard's 15 yields one; Expert's 14 yields one. This follows the current rules rather than a difficulty-normalized rating scheme.

**Expert limitation:** Expert has no allowance above the retained solution length. Playing that route uses 100% of the allowance and earns one star. A better route may improve the rating if the witness was not optimal. If the allowance is a proven minimum, more than one star is mathematically unattainable under these thresholds. The implementation does not adjust for that conflict.

The victory popup shows the current win's stars. The level tile keeps the highest saved rating, so a one-star replay does not replace an earlier three-star record. Earned tile stars use the glowing gold sprite; unearned positions remain gray. Goal diamonds are a separate row beneath them.

<a id="chapter-20"></a>

# 20. Goal diamonds and reward presentation

Level tiles have up to three cyan/gray diamond positions beneath the gold-star row. The number of filled positions reflects the saved earned-goal count; it is not an additional gold-star rating. A tile fills positions from left to right by count, so a filled first diamond is not a precise declaration that the first panel row was the goal earned. The panel rows identify the actual individual goals.

On the floating goal panel, a reached or previously earned goal uses a filled diamond and cyan text matching the icon family. A pending goal uses an unfilled diamond and neutral text. A goal missed in the current attempt uses an unfilled diamond and red text. Unfilled diamond opacity is 165/255. Previously earned goals remain shown as earned even if the current replay does not satisfy them again.

The panel no longer prints DONE and MISSED as status prefixes. An old explanatory heading still says “OPTIONAL GOALS · Stars saved on a win,” although the displayed secondary-goal indicators are diamonds. That wording is a presentation inconsistency, not an additional reward mechanic.

No score, currency, extra moves, or extra gold stars are awarded by the goal-achievement system. There is no integrated special all-goals completion celebration or dedicated automatic goal sound. The inspected saving behavior does not trigger an additional connected player reward or celebration.

The victory popup displays **+100** coins and **+5** gems on two reward cards. These values are fixed presentation placeholders. No wallet balance is credited, no inventory is updated, and no repeat-reward economy is implemented. They are not a payout derived from goals, stars, score, or difficulty. The current victory popup does not include a separate per-goal achievement breakdown.

<a id="chapter-21"></a>

# 21. Victory and level-completion sequence

The moment an accepted move connects every playable cube, the game sets the outcome to a win before checking exhausted moves. It prevents further painting, freezes the active-play result clock, evaluates optional goals, records new achievements and better completion time where tracking is available, records the lowest winning move count/highest stars, and saves completion. The next level in that same category becomes available even if no optional goal was achieved.

The final recolor wave is allowed to present. The board then performs its win transition: scale to 72% of its resting size, add a 12° tilt and 100° turn over 0.8 seconds, and begin a slower continuing rotation. The result popup is revealed after the last wave duration plus that 0.8-second transition plus the configured 0.45-second result delay.

The large modal says LEVEL COMPLETE! and identifies the level. Its statistics are MOVES, BEST, and TIME ELAPSED. BEST means the saved fewest winning moves for a numbered level; for custom play it falls back to this attempt. MOVES animates its numerator and time counts up from zero for presentation; the denominator and actual result remain fixed. It shows the present win's gold stars and the two placeholder reward cards.

Laurels rise from behind the banner, stars pop in sequentially, side confetti plays once, reward sparkles flicker/spin, and the crown's glow gently pulses and rotates. Decorative animation does not add moves, time, or points to the completed attempt. Result sound is scheduled after the color wave; music remains a ducked gameplay track rather than switching to a separate victory song.

| Action | Result |
| --- | --- |
| NEXT LEVEL | Starts the next numbered level in the same mode/difficulty when below 100 |
| REPLAY | Starts a new attempt at the same level; numbered definition and permanent records remain |
| HOME | Returns to the main menu |
| LEVELS | Opens category level selection |
| Primary LEVELS at Level 100 | Opens selection; no Level 101 is generated; the duplicate lower LEVELS action is hidden |

A worse replay still shows its own current stars but does not reduce stored stars or remove diamonds. A better move count replaces the best-moves record. A faster recorded win replaces the best time. Newly achieved goals add to the saved set. Winning with zero, some, or all goals never changes the basic unlock condition.

For a custom win, NEXT LEVEL currently enters the older sequential campaign described in Chapter 5 instead of producing a new numbered custom category. This distinction should be considered when demonstrating the product to a client.

<a id="chapter-22"></a>

# 22. Failure and recovery

The implemented gameplay loss condition is using the full move allowance while at least one playable cube remains outside the active connected component. A nearly complete board can lose. A Speed Run deadline is only an optional-goal failure, not a time-limit loss for the level. There is no life system, damage model, or paid extra-move mechanic.

Once loss is set, painting stops and current-attempt goal rewards are not granted. The final color wave is followed by the result delay, then OUT OF MOVES appears. The result details include the level where applicable, final score, moves used / allowance, and actual captured / total cubes with a percentage. The main action becomes PLAY AGAIN; LEVELS and MAIN MENU provide navigation. Victory decorations and reward cards do not appear on this loss screen.

Retrying a numbered level restores its saved original geometry, initial colors, allowance, and goals, and resets this attempt's moves, score, time, and temporary goal progress. Previous completed-level records, stars, and earned diamonds are untouched. A lost attempt is not available through Continue. The current failure score is a result of that attempt, not a newly saved high score.

Leaving a still-playable game through Home is not automatically a loss; it normally saves a resumable attempt. Cancelling preparation is not a recorded won/lost catalog result. Generation or saved-data errors are loading failures with TRY AGAIN and BACK, not move-limit failures. Retry cannot necessarily repair an unsupported preserved definition because existing definitions are deliberately not overwritten.

<a id="chapter-23"></a>

# 23. Restart, replay, Continue, and abandonment compared

| Action | Board and allowance | Attempt counters | Permanent numbered records | View |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Restart active numbered level | Reload saved initial board, colors, seed, budget and goals | Reset moves, score, clock and temporary goals | Keep completion, bests, stars and earned diamonds | Reframe and replay entrance |
| Replay completed numbered level | Same preserved definition | Fresh attempt | Can improve bests or add missing achievements; never subtract old ones | Fresh framing |
| Continue unfinished game | Restore current changed geometry/colors, budget and selection | Keep saved moves, score, clock and compatible goal history | Existing records unchanged until a win | Restore saved camera subject to safety limits |
| Start another numbered level | Its own saved definition or newly prepared one | Fresh attempt | Other catalog records remain | Fit the new object |
| Return Home during valid play | Keep board in one resumable checkpoint | Keep saved attempt values | No new completion award | Menu preview optional |
| Restart custom game | Regenerate under selected settings; not immutable replay | Fresh attempt | No numbered completion or goal award | Reframe |
| Return Home after win/loss | Finished checkpoint is not resumable; completed-session save is deleted on return | No playable Continue for that result | Won catalog records remain | Main menu |
| Leave loading | Cancel pending work; a cancelled Continue keeps its previous saved attempt | New unprepared attempt is not made playable | No completion award | Back to navigation |

One session slot means that starting a different puzzle abandons the previous resumable slot when the new attempt is saved. The same-level restart prompt protects the specific already saved numbered identity, not every possible replacement. Preferences and permanent catalog entries survive ordinary restarts.

Closing or backgrounding the app requests a checkpoint save. A forced process termination before a save finishes is not guaranteed to retain the latest fraction of a second or interaction. Continue is conditional on the checkpoint passing validity checks: supported state and dimensions, consistent identity/mode, nonnegative counters/time, remaining moves, valid colors, connected board data, and an incomplete board.

If Continue fails to restore a usable board, it returns to navigation and disables availability instead of deliberately showing an empty playable scene. This check does not supply cloud backup or multiple-session history.

<a id="chapter-24"></a>

# 24. Custom games

CUSTOM opens GAME SETUP. Playable custom modes are RANDOM SHAPE and FULL GRID. The screen also offers FRUITS, but selecting START GAME for that mode displays COMING SOON and does not launch a fruit puzzle. Its selection is remembered as a preference only.

Available sizes are **6, 8, 10, 12, 15, 18, and 20** along each dimension. Available difficulty names are EASY, NORMAL, HARD, PERFECT. The last mode, size, difficulty, and fruit preference are saved when changed. With no existing preferences the scene/custom setup starts in the Normal allowance tier; the numbered level selector independently defaults to Full Grid/Easy.

The fruit menu lists RANDOM FRUIT, APPLE, BANANA, ORANGE, PEAR, STRAWBERRY, GRAPES, PINEAPPLE, LEMON, and MANGO. Selecting any of these does not make a playable custom fruit game. Only Apple/Pear/Orange/Banana have active numbered Items silhouettes. The additional labels must not be presented as completed content.

Custom puzzles use the same flood rules, score calculation, solver/budget tiers, controls, timer, pause, and win/loss presentation. They can be continued as the single unfinished session. They do not create immutable numbered definitions or numbered optional goals, and do not unlock catalog levels or keep permanent catalog ratings. A victory can display attempt stars without persisting them as a numbered record.

Under the current Random Shape setting, restarting custom play creates a fresh seed and usually a new shape. Full Grid restarts keep the same shell shape at that size but reroll colors. The custom solver recovery allowance described in Chapter 13 can be used if verified preparation is unavailable. NEXT LEVEL after a custom win enters the older campaign route, so it should not be described as a numbered-level custom progression system.

<a id="chapter-25"></a>

# 25. Gameplay interface

| Element | Meaning and interaction |
| --- | --- |
| HOME | Opens the quit-to-home confirmation; does not immediately discard play |
| PAUSE | Opens PAUSED with Resume, Restart, Main Menu and Settings |
| Center title | LEVEL n for numbered/campaign play; FLOOD FILL 3D for ordinary custom play |
| MOVES | Accepted moves used / allowance; temporarily unavailable/calculating during preparation |
| TIME ELAPSED | Active-play clock, refreshed as seconds change; not capture percentage |
| SCORE | Current attempt score, formatted with thousands separators |
| Thin capture bar | Fraction of playable cubes in the latest active connected component; animates to each accepted result |
| Six palette buttons | Select paint; selected paint enlarges and its own button becomes non-interactable |
| Touch/click instruction | “Choose a color to expand your connected region.” The actual rules also allow working on another region. |
| GOALS | Shows permanently earned / assigned optional goals for a numbered level; opens/closes the floating panel |
| Floating goal rows | Instructions, supported progress counters, diamond state and completed/pending/missed color |
| FPS overlay | Editor/development feedback, not a normal release-game performance guarantee |

The palette's selected button grows to approximately 1.12 times its ordinary scale over 0.16 seconds. Button availability follows whether the game can accept input. Choosing paint by itself does not identify a permanent starting region; the next clicked cube identifies what will be recolored.

The goals panel refreshes progress while open, approximately every 0.2 seconds as well as on relevant changes. Big Capture displays best / target; Combo displays qualifying moves / count; Limited Color displays selections / allowance; early reach goals display cubes / target; Speed Run shows elapsed time. Final-color goals can remain pending until victory because merely choosing the correct color is not their completion condition.

The panel opens above the board rather than replacing it. The trigger can close it again. An outside tap closes and is consumed; an outside drag exceeding 12 pixels closes it and remains a camera gesture. With no assigned valid goals, the numbered GOALS control can show GOALS — and be non-interactable. Custom play does not expose numbered goal tracking.

The HUD and menus fit a reference layout to the safe screen area, accounting for notches and aspect changes. This does not constitute a verified accessibility mode: no separate color-blind palette, remappable controls, localization selector, or screen-reader flow was established by this inspection.

