Pause is an interaction/timer pause, **not a global freeze of every animation**. The inspected pause action does not set global game time to zero. An already running color wave can finish behind the menu, decorative effects can continue, and music can continue. This is distinct from operating-system application suspension, which pauses the audio sources and suspends the gameplay clock.

The authored interface has no active shop, wallet, inventory, level editor for players, online account, multiplayer lobby, paid revive, or extra-move purchase screen. Asset libraries or package dependencies do not establish those features.

<a id="chapter-27"></a>

# 27. Audio experience and music transitions

The game uses looping menu music and three gameplay intensity tracks. Music is chosen by remaining moves, not by capture percentage or elapsed time.

| Priority | Condition during gameplay | Track state |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First | Remaining moves ≤ 5 | High intensity |
| Second | Otherwise, remaining moves ≤ 50% of the starting allowance | Medium intensity |
| Otherwise | More moves remain | Normal intensity |

High intensity takes priority over the percentage rule. For an allowance of 20, 20–11 remaining uses Normal, 10–6 uses Medium, and 5–0 uses High. A short level with five or fewer moves begins in High even if no move has been spent. If dynamic music is disabled by development configuration, gameplay remains on Normal; that switch is not a separate player Settings control.

The menu track has an authored volume multiplier of 0.8; each gameplay track uses 0.7. Menu fade-in and fade-out are 1.5 seconds. Gameplay crossfades are 1.75 seconds, with a 0.4-second transition cooldown; the high-intensity condition can override the ordinary cooldown. Two music voices overlap during a crossfade, rather than starting unlimited tracks. A missing high track can fall back to Medium or Normal, and a missing Medium can fall back to Normal; gameplay does not substitute menu music as its fallback.

On win or loss, the current gameplay music is reduced to 30% of its normal gain while a result cue is scheduled after the recolor wave. There is **no separately configured victory or failure music track**. In-game Pause currently leaves background music running; app suspension pauses it and remembers which sources were playing for resume.

### Interaction and wave sounds

Assigned cues cover ordinary buttons, confirm/back/cancel, toggles, slider feedback, palette selection, level start, restart, pause/resume, and victory/failure. Typical UI sounds have 0.08-second minimum spacing and at most two overlapping copies; sliders use quieter feedback with 0.12-second minimum spacing and one copy. Some types use slightly randomized pitch without influencing puzzle-generation randomness.

The active recolor sound follows the wave as a short repeating section of the assigned color-change clip. Its repeat section is 0.16 seconds, starts after a 0.105-second clip offset, and speeds from 0.8 to 1.65 times its configured pitch across the wave. It fades near the end, using up to 0.2 seconds and at most 40% of a very short wave. A new move replaces the previous wave voice. This prevents one sound per cube on a large object.

The shared effects pool is configured for 12 voices, with per-sound concurrency and rate limits; flood sounds also have a configured concurrency limit of two. This bounds overlap rather than scaling audio voices with cube count.

There are assigned legacy progress and large-capture clips and older progress thresholds in the audio configuration, but the current automatic flood path uses the synchronized loop rather than scheduling those extra cues on each threshold. Do not promise a separate audible large-capture reward merely because its clip is assigned. Popup-open, popup-close, and flood-end slots currently have no clips. There is no integrated automatic goal-achievement sound. Development preview buttons can play configured cues separately and are not evidence of normal gameplay triggers.

