# 28. Settings and preference persistence

| Setting | Range / choice | New-install configured value | Player effect |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| MASTER VOLUME | 0–100% | 100% | Scales all audible game sound |
| MUSIC VOLUME | 0–100% | 65% | Scales background music before its track multiplier/result duck |
| SOUND EFFECTS | 0–100% | 80% | Scales interaction and gameplay cues |
| MUSIC ENABLED | On / off | On | Mutes/restores music without overwriting its stored slider value |
| SOUND EFFECTS ENABLED | On / off | On | Mutes/restores effects without overwriting their stored slider value |
| SHOW CURRENT BLOCK | On / off | Off | Uses the available current board as the menu centerpiece rather than the decorative cluster |

Audio changes apply immediately. Their values are saved on audio-manager shutdown, application pause, focus loss, or quit. The inspected slider/toggle setters do not individually force a disk save for every movement. Therefore persistence after a normal lifecycle save is supported; persistence after an abrupt kill immediately following a slider movement is not guaranteed. Setup and SHOW CURRENT BLOCK preferences are explicitly saved when changed.

Approximate steady-state music gain is **Master × Music × track multiplier**, with a further result factor of 0.3 after victory/failure and transition gain during a fade. At configured defaults, ordinary gameplay music has gain 1 × 0.65 × 0.7 = 0.455 before clip content and any mixing details. This is not a measured perceived-loudness percentage.

Audio level zero means silence. The mixer represents very low/zero gain as −80 dB; positive gain uses 20 × log10(gain). Muting a channel preserves its slider, so re-enabling music can restore the earlier 65% or the player's chosen value. The effects-enable toggle has a special confirmation that can be audible while turning its own channel off/on, still respecting Master and the stored effects level.

These preferences apply across all modes. There is no player-facing camera-sensitivity slider, graphics-quality selector, vibration option, language selector, or reset-progress button in the inspected Settings screen. The explanatory line about orbit, pan, and zoom is information, not a toggle that enables those controls.

