<a id="chapter-30"></a>

# 30. Local saves and persistence

Progress is local to the application/device. No account login, cloud synchronization, server validation, shared cross-device progression, or Remote Config integration was established in active gameplay. Internet access is not required by the inspected puzzle, generation, solver, or save flows. Installed packages do not create an implemented store or cloud feature by themselves.

| Saved information | Purpose | When retained or reset |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Numbered completion and unlock basis | Remembers won identities; makes later levels available | Retained across app restarts; ordinary replay does not erase it |
| Best gold stars and fewest winning moves | Preserves improved efficiency | Updated only when a win improves the relevant record |
| Best recorded numbered completion time | Preserves faster successful play | Saved with numbered goal-result processing where the tracker is available |
| Goal achievement identities | Keeps earned diamonds without duplicates | Added on successful qualifying wins; never removed by a worse replay |
| Immutable numbered definition | Exact initial occupancy/colors, palette, seed, budget, witness and goals | Created on first successful preparation; restored on replay |
| One unfinished checkpoint | Current colors/active flags, moves, score, time, mode/difficulty, selected paint, camera and compatible goal history | Updated after color choices, accepted nonterminal moves, result handling, Home and lifecycle saves |
| Setup/menu preferences | Custom choices and SHOW CURRENT BLOCK | Saved when changed |
| Audio preferences | Volumes and enable toggles | Lifecycle/shutdown saves described in Chapter 28 |
| Older campaign position | Supports the indirect legacy route | Advances on campaign wins |
| Developer solver history | QA attempts, plans, times and outcomes | Separate Editor-side history, not player catalog progression |

The numbered definition includes a compact representation of the initial cubes and an integrity check. Loading checks its identity, versions, palette, valid geometry, allowance, winning witness, and demonstrable goals. Definitions are cached in memory with an eight-level limit; this is a loading optimization, not a limit of eight saved levels.

Checkpoint saving first writes a temporary file and copies it to the live session path. This reduces partial-write exposure but is not a cloud backup or a guaranteed recovery journal. Invalid/missing session data disables Continue rather than inventing progress. Completed or exhausted sessions are rejected for Continue even if a result-state checkpoint remains until menu return deletes it.

A saved numbered definition that is damaged or unsupported is preserved and reported as an error. It is not overwritten automatically. A damaged catalog can fall back to an empty catalog, so unlocking may appear reset; there is no in-game repair/import tool. Physical storage failures can prevent persistence even when an in-memory win was valid.

There is no visible player reset-progress control, multi-profile selection, cross-device transfer, or general restore-from-backup screen. Removing or changing local application data outside the game can lose progress. This document does not instruct clients to delete their saves as normal recovery.

<a id="chapter-31"></a>

# 31. Loading, cancellation, and performance behavior

First-time numbered preparation can take longer because it must generate geometry/colors, build a region model, demonstrate a solution, calculate an allowance, validate goal targets, and save the definition. A preserved level can restore its definition directly instead. Continue restores the current changed board and uses the same readiness gate before play appears.

During preparation, the loading screen covers the board, shows a meaningful phase message, and disables painting/camera input. Cube acquisition and color setup can be spread across frames, yielding after roughly 4 ms of cooperative main-thread work. That is a scheduling target, not a guarantee that no individual operation or logical-generation phase exceeds 4 ms.

Search work runs away from the main presentation thread, with one solver worker admitted at a time. Restart or Back cancels obsolete work; old results are prevented from appearing over a newer request. This avoids deliberately allowing several rapid restarts to compete indefinitely for solver resources.

After the board and budget are ready, the hidden view is prepared, cube physics positions are synchronized, and the game retains an additional 0.5-second readiness buffer before showing the entrance. The entrance has its own 0.55-second interaction lock. These are in addition to actual work, not a fake fixed loading timer or a misleading percentage.

Saved-level caching reduces repeated reads, pooled cubes reduce repeated object creation, surface-only geometry reduces the number of playable/displayed cubes, and size simplification makes large irregular objects less intricate. Color materials are shared for resting palette states; temporary color transitions still need their own animated presentation. These mechanisms are performance intentions, not a promise of a specific phone frame rate.

If irregular geometry cannot validate, a hollow-cube fallback is available. If the solver cannot provide a publishable numbered witness, preparation ends with an error and TRY AGAIN/BACK. Custom play has a conservative emergency allowance. Optional-goal generation can return fewer goals rather than blocking an otherwise valid numbered puzzle.

Previously recorded desktop verification and performance notes exist, but they use specific fixtures, old tuning, and development conditions. This document makes no new Android/iOS FPS, memory, battery, or loading-time claim. The currently saved camera clearance also differs from an older framing note, so that older visual-performance result must not be presented as a current exact view guarantee.

<a id="chapter-32"></a>

# 32. Development and testing tools

The Unity Editor provides **Tools / Flood Fill / Auto Solver** and **Tools / Flood Fill / Solver Summary**. These are development/QA windows, not buttons offered to ordinary players in a release build. The runtime preparation solver remains part of the game; the interactive developer windows do not.

Auto Solver can connect to the active playable board, analyze it, report whether its proposed route was validated, play the route through normal game interactions, pause/resume, step, stop, restart the current level, or move to the next available numbered level. It displays the current category/level, captures, selected color, remaining moves, elapsed level time, search statistics, and planned sequence. Playback speed and visual-transition controls support debugging; temporary visual overrides are restored on cleanup.

Checking **Auto Start Solver** alone does not start a run. A user must press **Start Auto Solve** to arm the session. After that, Auto Start Solver can analyze/play newly ready levels, and **Auto Next Level** can advance after a win. Advancement is within the category and stops at Level 100; a failed level does not automatically count as complete. Restart and Next use the game's own level flow rather than merely swapping displayed numbers.

A tool route can be valid yet **over budget**. In that case normal gameplay can stop at its move limit before the tool finishes. This does not prove the board has no legal within-budget solution; a preserved level may already contain a shorter witness. The UI and summary distinguish validated, optimal, failed, and over-budget analysis where available.

Solver Summary groups rows by EASY, MEDIUM, HARD, and EXPERT and offers All levels, Attempt history, and Completed levels views. It records in-progress and failed attempts as well as wins. Fields include mode/type/level, status, completed flag, tries to first win, total attempts/losses/wins, moves used, latest planned moves required, given moves, best winning moves, seed, dimensions, playable cube count, capture result, search time, active level time, and solver-session wall time. Search time, active-play time, and solver wall time are different measurements.

One try means a board run on which automated solving starts. Stopping and resuming that same board is not another try; restarting creates another. Analysis alone does not create a play attempt. History updates while running, including periodic approximately one-second updates and move/outcome changes. Earlier unlogged attempts cannot be reconstructed. “Moves required” is the latest analyzed plan length from the analysis position, not a proven minimum and not necessarily the full initial-level route length.

Other Editor tools provide scene/UI installation, authored-screen previews, solver profiling, audio previews, and regression checks for generation, budgets, actual wins/losses, camera framing, Continue, goals, result presentation, and navigation. Audio preview controls can play configured cues that have no normal automatic trigger. The FPS overlay is enabled for Editor/development builds and hidden for normal release builds; it is a debugging indicator rather than a certified performance target. Removed wave-splash preview/installation tools are no longer part of the project.

