Notice that the first click is not the original cyan corner. That is legal free-region play. Its active component contains 13 cubes; the previously active corner is not among them, so “new” is 13, not 12. This is why capture should not be explained as a permanent cumulative collection counter.

The Big Capture goal is provisionally reached on move 1. The 25% goal is reached on move 4 because 42 ≥ 38. At move 14 the object is one orange component, 152/152 is truly 100%, six moves remain, and the level is won. The Move Saver target is satisfied because 14 ≤ 17. Normal music changes to Medium when the remaining count reaches 10 at move 10; the run wins with six left, so it never enters the High track. Result presentation ducks that Medium music and plays the completion cue after the wave.

The illustrated final score is **2,584**, with no extra win, time, goal, or spare-move bonus. The present gold rating is **three stars**, because 14/20 = 0.70. All three goal identities become permanently earned, the category's Level 2 becomes available, and Level 1's completion/best-move record is saved. Its actual active-play duration, whatever the player took, is saved for best-time comparison; it cannot be calculated from the move list alone. The popup's +100 and +5 remain decorative placeholders, not additions to 2,584 or a wallet.

### A replay that improves missing achievements

A deliberately less efficient but valid first attempt can add two unproductive moves at the starting cyan corner: recolor it orange, then cyan again. Its initial neighbors contain no orange, so the first change does not merge anything; the second restores the original board colors. Both are legal color changes and spend moves, but acquire no new active cubes and score zero. The preserved 14-move route can then still be followed.

This attempt wins in **16 moves**. Its early four moves contain the two delays and only the first two witness moves, reaching 20/152, so it misses the 38-cube deadline. It still wins within 17 and makes a 9+ capture, earning two diamonds. Its rating is two stars because 16/20 = 0.80. With the same illustrative scoring draws on the productive moves it can still total 2,584, showing why score does not determine stars.

On replay, follow the original 14-move route without the two delays. The third diamond is added, the saved rating increases from two to three, and best moves improves from 16 to 14. No second copy of the already earned goals is awarded. A later 19-move win would show one star for that attempt but would not erase the saved three stars or diamonds. A failed attempt would add none.


